  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "automated-microscopy-2e31",
  "summary": "Current work separates cell abundance, intrinsic state, and neighbor spillover when attributing spatial transcriptomic measurements to cells.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is attributing spatially resolved measurements to their sources: which cell, which state, and which neighborhood. ARCADE separates cell-type abundance from continuous intratype transcriptional state, while DisCell is a generative model that partitions intrinsic cell state, niche response, and neighbor spillover. Tracel reassigns transcripts or bin counts to cells via two-stage optimal transport against matched scRNA-seq references, and spOT-NMF performs reference-free deconvolution with proximity-aware niche mapping. TileScope moves beyond colocalization by quantifying gene and program relationships within tissue regions through enrichment, correlation, cross-correlation and boundary metrics. PixAQ applies a related attribution approach to single-cell protein quantification in imaging mass cytometry, using marker-specific probabilistic pixel assignment rather than shared masks. The newer contribution is modeling state and neighborhood effects as separable components rather than confounded readouts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-09"
}
